  "statement": "You have `n` ropes with integer lengths and need `k` pieces of **equal real length** `L`, each cut from a single rope (a rope of length `x` yields `⌊x / L⌋` pieces). Find the largest real `L` for which at least `k` pieces can be obtained.\n\nTo avoid printing decimals, print `⌊100 · L⌋`, that is `L` rounded **down** to hundredths, written as an integer (for example `L = 3.14159` prints `314`).\n\n**Input.** First line `n k`. Second line the `n` lengths.\n\n**Output.** One integer.\n\n**Example.**\n\nInput:\n```\n4 11\n802 743 457 539\n```\nOutput:\n```\n20050\n```",
  "constraints": [
    "1 ≤ n ≤ 1000",
    "1 ≤ length ≤ 10^6",
    "1 ≤ k ≤ sum of all lengths (so a piece of length 1 always works)"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 1000,
