  "hints": [
    "For a fixed length `L`, how many pieces do you get? Is that count increasing or decreasing in `L`?",
    "The piece count is non-increasing in `L`, so 'at least k pieces' is a monotone predicate; a binary search on `L` works.",
    "You never need floating point: `L >= c/100` is checked with the integer `sum(len * 100 // c)`; search the largest integer `c`."
  ],
  "editorial": "The number of pieces `f(L) = sum floor(len_i / L)` is non-increasing in `L`, so feasibility `f(L) >= k` is `T…T F…F`: binary search the largest `L`. The maximum `L*` is itself feasible because `f` is left-continuous at that point (it equals `len_i / j` for some `i, j`).\n\nThe required output is `floor(100 · L*)`. For an integer `c`, `c/100 <= L*` iff `c` is feasible, so the answer is the **largest integer `c` with `sum(floor(100 · len_i / c)) >= k`**. That turns a floating-point search into an exact integer one: `c` ranges up to `100 · max(len)`, about 27 iterations of an `O(n)` check, with 64-bit counters (`100 · 10^6 · 1000` fits easily).\n\nIf the statement asked for real output you would loop a fixed ~60 to 100 times on `doubles` and print with fixed precision; the integer reformulation here removes any epsilon risk.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "starter": {
    "python": "import sys\n\n# Largest equal piece length, in hundredths.\ndata = sys.stdin.read().split()\n# your code here\n",
